Id: stor007w
Severity: WARN
Category: filesystem
Check: check_storage
Fix: Add '* hard core 0' to limits.conf, or install systemd-coredump.
References: core(5), limits.conf(5)
Controls: CIS 4.1; NIST CM-6; ISO 8.9; CE Secure configuration

When a program crashes the kernel writes its memory to a core file
(kernel.core_pattern names it, and it is not handed to a program such
as systemd-coredump or apport), and no '* hard core 0' in
/etc/security/limits.conf limits that. A program's memory can hold
passwords, keys and private data, and the core file is left in its
working directory with the program user's permissions, where it may be
read or copied long after.

To fix it, add '* hard core 0' to /etc/security/limits.conf, or install
systemd-coredump, which keeps dumps in /var/lib/systemd/coredump where
only root reads them and removes them after a while. fs.suid_dumpable
(lin027w) covers setuid programs.
